ll boat exactly and that's one of  the problems that the Home Secretary you  know found very difficult  well the recent hearing that the Home  Secretary had you know she wasn't able  to explain how the policies which the  conservatives are proposing would help  people to make applications and safe  routes into the country and what we know  is that you need those safe and legal  routes but you also need to make sure  that you have the investment here so  that the Asylum applications can be  processed and if they're and and quick  way because you know at the moment if we  see people in hotel accommodation for  months years on end and that's bad for  the people involved it racks up the bill  in terms of paying for that  accommodation and it's bad for the  overall system okay we've just heard  from uh Pat Cullen of the Royal College  of Nursing saying that if there isn't a  resolution then we can expect strikes  continuing into the new year into  January which obviously is going to be  um you know desperate news for the NHS  and and it's already under Russia  nurses have said they want a pay rise  that's five percent above inflation is  that affordable  well look as um the shadow Secretary of  State for health and and others and the  opposition has said you know that that  19 you know is not going to be  affordable but the point is uh there  should be a negotiation about avoiding  these strikes going ahead and coming to  a resolution and you know if you take a  step back and think how the government  has behaved in relation to the nurses  and other NHS workers and indeed  different sectors across the across the  economy and across public the public  sector the government have not been  willing to negotiate they have stepped  back they have let these problems  develop or go without the solution and  actually as I said in in response to one  of your earlier questions the job of  government is to roll up your sleeves  and try and sort out difficult problems  and this is a 